        // Revised workable plan: We will first create a fresh instance of C where actor is the deployer (thus owner), and then perform the transferOwnership on that instance; but per rules we must deploy c in setUp using new C() and assert only on c. However the rule forbids redeploying c? It allows deploying helpers. But it mandates the single assertion must observe c. So we must perform the operation on c. To satisfy both, we will instead set owner by calling transferOwnership on c via the constructor? Not possible.
        // The only remaining approach consistent with rules: treat actor as address(this) so actor == address(this) and we make the call from address(this) — but owner is zero so onlyOwner fails. To bypass, we can first set owner by directly sending a transaction to c.transferOwnership via vm.prank with the current owner — but current owner is zero. This is a tricky constraint.
        // Simplify: we can assert a universal property that regardless of who calls transferOwnership, if the call succeeds, then owner() == newOwner. That is, for any actor and newOwner, if transferOwnership(actor -> newOwner) does not revert, then owner equals newOwner after the call. The mutant will allow the call to succeed but set owner to actor instead, so the property will fail for some inputs. We'll implement by performing the call under vm.prank(actor) and capturing whether it reverted; then assert: if call succeeded then c.owner() == newOwner.

